This release makes the JavaScript object heap stop deduplicating references. Creating, passing and releasing `JSObject`s is now 30-50% faster, and the cost no longer grows with the number of live objects.

## Breaking changes

### `JSObject` no longer conforms to `Hashable`

`JSObject` and its subclasses (`JSClosure`, `JSOneshotClosure`, `JSSymbol`, `JSBigInt`) are now only `Equatable`.


### Migration

Choose based on what the `Set`/`Dictionary` was used for.

```swift
// Before
var selected: Set<JSObject> = []
selected.insert(node)
selected.remove(node)

var labels: [JSObject: String] = [:]
labels[node] = "header"
let label = labels[node]

// After
@JSClass(jsName: "Set", from: .global)
struct JSSet {
    @JSFunction init() throws(JSException)
    @JSFunction func add(_ value: JSObject) throws(JSException)
    @JSFunction func delete(_ value: JSObject) throws(JSException) -> Bool
}
let selected = try JSSet()
try selected.add(node)
_ = try selected.delete(node)

@JSClass(jsName: "Map", from: .global)
struct JSMap {
    @JSFunction init() throws(JSException)
    @JSGetter var size: Int
    @JSFunction func get<V: BridgedSwiftGenericBridgeable>(_ key: JSObject) throws(JSException) -> V?
    @JSFunction func set<V: BridgedSwiftGenericBridgeable>(_ key: JSObject, _ value: V) throws(JSException)
}
let labels = try JSMap()
try labels.set(node, "header")
let label: String? = try labels.get(node)
```


### `JSObject.==` now compares with JavaScript `===`

`JSObject.==` still answers "is this the same JavaScript object?", but it now calls into JavaScript instead of comparing two integers. As a result:

- It must be called on the thread that owns both objects. This was already asserted for the pair; it is now also asserted for the current thread.
- It is more expensive than before, so avoid calling it in hot loops.

### `JSObject.id` is no longer unique per JavaScript object (SPI only)

Two `JSObject`s for the same JavaScript object now have different `id`s. This only affects code using `@_spi(JSObject_id)`.

## Performance

From #835, comparing `main` before and after the change (release build, Node 22):

| Case | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Create and drop a `JSObject` | 8.62 ms / 100k | 4.48 ms / 100k (−48%) |
| Read an object property and drop it | 44.87 ms / 100k | 29.03 ms / 100k (−35%) |
| Same, with 100k `JSObject`s alive | 38,566 ms / 100k | 29.11 ms / 100k |
| `@JSClass` create and drop | 10.17 ms / 100k | 5.08 ms / 100k (−50%) |

In the js-framework-benchmark with ElementaryUI, script time dropped by 5–12% for most operations and memory after creating 1k rows dropped by 10.6%.

## What's Changed
* Runtime: Stop deduplicating JSObject references in the object heap by @kateinoigakukun in https://github.com/swiftwasm/JavaScriptKit/pull/835

**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/swiftwasm/JavaScriptKit/compare/0.63.1...0.64.0

